Backend – Express.js

The backend is a simple Express API.

It enables JSON parsing and CORS and provides an API endpoint:

text
GET /api/message

Example response:

json
{
  "message": "Hello from chaicode server"
}

The backend obtains its port from an environment variable:

javascript
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 4000;

The application listens on all network interfaces:

javascript
app.listen(PORT, "0.0.0.0", () => {
  console.log(`Server is running on port ${PORT}`);
});

Using `0.0.0.0` allows the application to receive traffic from outside the container.


Backend Dockerfile

The backend container uses Node.js Alpine.

Example:

dockerfile
FROM node:22-alpine

WORKDIR /app

COPY package*.json ./

RUN npm install --production

COPY . .

EXPOSE 4000

CMD ["npm", "start"]

The Docker image can be built manually from the `server` directory:

bash
docker build -t express-server .

Run the backend image:

bash
docker run -p 4000:4000 express-server

Test it:

bash
curl http://localhost:4000/api/message



Frontend – React + Vite

The frontend is built with React and Vite.

The React application requests data from:

text
/api/message

Example:

javascript
fetch("/api/message")
  .then((response) => response.json())
  .then((data) => setMessage(data.message))
  .catch((error) => console.error(error));


During local development, Vite can proxy API requests to:

text
http://localhost:4000


Example Vite configuration:

javascript
export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [react()],
  server: {
    port: 3000,
    proxy: {
      "/api": {
        target: "http://localhost:4000",
        changeOrigin: true
      }
    }
  }
});

The development proxy is only used during local frontend development.

In the Docker deployment, Nginx handles API proxying.


Frontend Dockerfile

The frontend uses a **multi-stage Docker build**.

The first stage uses Node.js to build the React application.

The second stage uses Nginx to serve the generated files.

dockerfile
FROM node:22-alpine AS build

WORKDIR /app

COPY package*.json ./

RUN npm install

COPY . .

RUN npm run build


FROM nginx:stable-alpine

RUN rm -rf /usr/share/nginx/html/*

COPY --from=build /app/dist /usr/share/nginx/html

COPY nginx.conf /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf

EXPOSE 80

CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]

Using a multi-stage build keeps build dependencies out of the final Nginx image.


Nginx Configuration

Nginx serves the React frontend and proxies API requests to the backend container.

Example:

nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name localhost;

    root /usr/share/nginx/html;
    index index.html;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
    }

    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://backend:4000/api/;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;

        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;

        proxy_connect_timeout 5s;
        proxy_read_timeout 60s;
    }

    gzip on;
    gzip_types text/plain application/json text/css application/javascript;
}

The important configuration is:

text
proxy_pass http://backend:4000/api/;

`backend` is the Docker Compose service name.

Docker's internal DNS resolves the service name to the correct backend container.


Environment Variables

Runtime configuration is separated from application source code.

Example:

env
PORT=4000
NODE_ENV=production

An example file is provided:

text
server/.env.example


Create your local environment file:

bash
cp server/.env.example server/.env


The actual `.env` file should not be uploaded to GitHub.

Add it to `.gitignore`:

text
server/.env


Docker Compose can load the file using:

yaml
env_file:
  - ./server/.env


Verify variables inside the running container:

bash
docker exec fullstack_backend printenv | grep -E 'PORT|NODE_ENV'


Docker Compose

Docker Compose manages both application containers.

Example configuration:

yaml
services:

  backend:
    build:
      context: ./server
      dockerfile: Dockerfile

    container_name: fullstack_backend

    ports:
      - "4000:4000"

    env_file:
      - ./server/.env

    restart: unless-stopped


  client:
    build:
      context: ./client
      dockerfile: Dockerfile

    container_name: fullstack_frontend

    ports:
      - "8080:80"

    restart: unless-stopped

    depends_on:
      - backend


Port Mapping

text
Frontend:
Host 8080 → Container 80

Backend:
Host 4000 → Container 4000

The frontend originally used host port `80`, but it was changed to `8080` because port `80` was already being used on the Ubuntu host.


